<p>
  An automation rule queues a <strong>job</strong>
  for a <strong>pool</strong>: <em>Send cards to a runner</em>
  when a card arrives in a list, or <em>Work a list with a runner</em>, which sends the top
  card, one at a time. A runner in that pool pulls the job, or a session takes it with <code>slipdock claim-job B --pool loop</code>.
</p>
<p>
  Recurring agent work (a nightly sweep, the handbook's reconcile) lands in the board's
  <strong>Scheduled</strong>
  list, when it has one: a list in the <code>scheduled</code>
  category, after To Do. Nobody picks from it, people or agents asked what to do next. Its
  own <em>Work Scheduled with a runner</em>
  rules send each card on its day: when a list becomes Scheduled, every rule working the
  ready list gets one beside it, for the same pool. A Scheduled card still there after its
  due day is on its owner's <strong>Waiting on you</strong>.
</p>
<p>
  Small fixes and investigations that sweeps, findings triage and agents file wait in the
  <strong>Housekeeping</strong>
  list (category <code>housekeeping</code>). It gets the same rules beside the ready list's, <em>Work Housekeeping with a runner</em>, but each one backfills: it sends a card only while
  To Do and Scheduled have nothing that pool could start. Your own work always goes first.
  A Housekeeping card that turns out to need a design choice, or grows into feature work,
  comes back to you: the agent moves it to Triage with a <em>product</em>
  wait saying what grew, and builds nothing. With no Triage list on the board, it stays
  where it is, held by its wait until you answer.
  With the overseer on, each morning (07:00 unless you set another time) its card gets a
  digest of what Housekeeping worked, grouped by theme, naming only what needs you.
</p>
<p>
  The card moves into progress, and the <strong>bar locks</strong>: from then on only a
  person can change it. For a <em>Work a list with a runner</em> job the server makes the
  move the moment a runner claims it, to the board's doing list (the one the guide names),
  and the job's prompt names that list; for any other job the agent moves the card. A job is held, saying why, while the board is paused, its WIP
  limit is full (<em>WIP limits hold automated work</em>), another card is in progress, or
  its lane overlaps one.
</p>
<p>
  While the job is claimed or running, the card's Assignees show the <strong>loop</strong>:
  the runner and the job's kind (build or verify), linked to the job. It goes when the job
  ends. The agent doesn't assign the card to anyone, since it signs in with a person's token.
</p>
<.person_note>
  <strong>A batched board releases on a trigger, not on a merge.</strong>
  A card there is done when it merges; agents don't wait for its release. A release runs when
  a critical card merges, at the board's age cap, or when the owner presses <em>Release now</em>
  (<code>slipdock release B</code>): a runner's <code>release</code>
  job runs the board's release command, one release at a time. A command other than the
  default <code>gh workflow run</code>
  runs only on a machine whose <code>local.json</code>
  lists it under <code>release_commands</code>. A red release holds the next one and files a
  critical fix card: fix that card, and its merge releases; only the owner lifts a hold by
  hand. <em>Not shipped</em>
  counts from the release there, never from the merge, so a card awaiting release is not
  late.
</.person_note>
<.person_note>
  <strong>A pause is yours to lift.</strong>
  Agents may pause runner work (<code>slipdock pause B --reason "…"</code>), and an agent
  that sees it takes nothing. Only a person resumes it: <code>slipdock resume B</code>
  or the Runners panel.
</.person_note>
